Pins that each candidate target's own repository decides the outcome: inner/ is a separate git repository nested under the sandbox, and the OUTER repo's .gitignore lists inner/ so the outer repo ignores the whole directory. But git rev-parse --show-toplevel run from inner/ finds the inner repo first, not the outer one, so the guard checks nested.txt against the inner repo's own tracking (tracked, not ignored there) and denies -- proving the outer .gitignore entry for inner/ has no bearing on files the inner repo itself tracks.
